--- validation happens before anything expensive ---

  a two-character question    422  String should have at least 3 characters
  max_steps out of range      422  Input should be less than or equal to 12

--- POST /ask ---

    6.2s to the first byte and the last, because they are the same byte
  tools: query_warehouse, search_documents
  Revenue in 2024 Q3 was **$8,461,841.81**. The **Midwest decline** 

--- POST /ask/stream ---

   0.03s  accepted 
   2.23s  step     query_warehouse
   4.54s  step     search_documents
   6.50s  answer   Revenue in 2024 Q3 was **$8,461,841.81**. Th
   6.50s  done     

  first byte at 0.03s, last at 6.5s
  the plain endpoint took 6.2s and showed nothing until the end

Streaming did not make the work faster — both answers took about the same
time. What changed is that a person sees the system working at 0.03s instead
of staring at a spinner for 6 seconds, and §28.6 is about how much that
difference is worth.

Two details in that stream matter more than the mechanism.

The steps are events, not text fragments. A token stream is what a chat
interface wants; a *step* stream is what an agent should emit, because 'searching
the 2024 Q3 review' is information and a half-written sentence is not.

And the work runs on a thread while the endpoint stays async. The engine is
synchronous and takes seconds — §25.7 is what happens when you forget that —
so it goes to an executor and posts its progress back to the loop through a
queue.
